    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Matter is <span class=\"learn-keyword\">anything that has mass and occupies space</span>. Matter exists in three common physical states — <span class=\"learn-keyword\">solid, liquid, and gas</span> — and can change from one state to another through heating or cooling.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. States of Matter</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Solid</th><th>Liquid</th><th>Gas</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Shape</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Takes container shape</td><td>Takes container shape</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Volume</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Expands to fill container</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Compressibility</td><td>Not compressible</td><td>Slightly compressible</td><td>Highly compressible</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Particle arrangement</td><td>Closely packed, regular</td><td>Close, less regular</td><td>Far apart, random</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Particle movement</td><td>Vibrate in fixed positions</td><td>Slide past each other</td><td>Move freely at high speed</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Changes of State</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Melting:</strong> Solid → Liquid (absorbs heat). Temperature = melting point.</li>\n  <li><strong>Freezing:</strong> Liquid → Solid (releases heat). Temperature = freezing point.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vaporisation/Boiling:</strong> Liquid → Gas (absorbs heat). Temperature = boiling point.</li>\n  <li><strong>Condensation:</strong> Gas → Liquid (releases heat).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sublimation:</strong> Solid → Gas directly (e.g., iodine, dry ice, naphthalene).</li>\n  <li><strong>Deposition:</strong> Gas → Solid directly.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Elements, Compounds, and Mixtures</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Element:</strong> A pure substance made of only one type of atom (e.g., Fe, O₂, Au).</li>\n  <li><strong>Compound:</strong> A pure substance formed when two or more elements are chemically combined in a fixed ratio (e.g., H₂O, NaCl, CO₂).</li>\n  <li><strong>Mixture:</strong> Two or more substances physically combined (e.g., air, saltwater). Can be homogeneous (uniform) or heterogeneous (non-uniform).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Changes of state that absorb heat are endothermic (melting, vaporisation, sublimation). Those that release heat are exothermic (freezing, condensation, deposition). Sublimation examples: iodine, dry ice (solid CO₂), naphthalene (mothballs).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Matter exists as solid, liquid, or gas with distinct properties. Elements contain one atom type; compounds are chemically combined; mixtures are physically combined. Homogeneous mixtures are uniform; heterogeneous are non-uniform.\n</div>\n  ",
