    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Separation techniques</span> exploit differences in the physical properties of mixture components — such as particle size, boiling point, solubility, or density — to separate them without causing any chemical change.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Filtration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid. The mixture is poured through filter paper in a funnel. The liquid (filtrate) passes through; the solid (residue) remains.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> Separating sand from water; chalk from water.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Distillation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Simple distillation</strong> separates a soluble solid from a liquid, or two liquids with very different boiling points. The liquid is boiled, vapour condenses in the condenser, and pure liquid (distillate) is collected.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Fractional distillation</strong> separates liquids with similar boiling points using a fractionating column. Examples: separating ethanol from water; refining crude oil.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Crystallisation and Evaporation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Evaporation to dryness:</strong> Heats a solution until all solvent evaporates, leaving the solid. Suitable for thermally stable salts (e.g., NaCl).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Crystallisation:</strong> Solution heated then cooled slowly, allowing dissolved substance to form crystals. Example: obtaining copper sulphate crystals.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Sublimation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Separates a subliming solid from a non-subliming solid. The subliming substance vaporises on heating and re-solidifies on a cold surface. Example: separating iodine from sand; ammonium chloride from salt.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Chromatography</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Separates mixtures based on different rates of movement through a stationary phase carried by a mobile phase (solvent). Types: paper chromatography, thin-layer chromatography (TLC).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Rf value:</strong> Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent front.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Other Techniques</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Decantation:</strong> Carefully pouring off liquid from a settled solid.</li>\n  <li><strong>Centrifugation:</strong> High-speed spinning to separate fine particles from liquid.</li>\n  <li><strong>Magnetic separation:</strong> Using a magnet (e.g., iron filings from sand).</li>\n  <li><strong>Solvent extraction:</strong> Using a separating funnel for immiscible liquids.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Match the technique to the property: filtration = particle size; distillation = boiling point; crystallisation = solubility; chromatography = adsorption; sublimation = direct vaporisation. Know Rf = substance distance ÷ solvent front distance.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Separation techniques exploit physical property differences. Filtration (particle size), distillation (boiling point), crystallisation (solubility), sublimation (direct vaporisation), chromatography (differential movement), magnetic separation (magnetism), solvent extraction (immiscibility).\n</div>\n  ",
