    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">atom</span> is the smallest particle of an element that retains the chemical properties of that element. Modern atomic theory describes a dense nucleus surrounded by electrons in shells.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Subatomic Particles</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Charge</th><th>Relative Mass</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Proton</td><td>p⁺</td><td>+1</td><td>1</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Neutron</td><td>n⁰</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Nucleus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Electron</td><td>e⁻</td><td>−1</td><td>~1/1836 (negligible)</td><td>Electron shells</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Atomic Number and Mass Number</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Atomic number (Z):</strong> Number of protons. Defines the element. In a neutral atom, Z = number of electrons.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mass number (A):</strong> Protons + neutrons. A = Z + N.</li>\n  <li><strong>Number of neutrons:</strong> N = A − Z.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example: Carbon-12: Z = 6, A = 12, neutrons = 12 − 6 = 6.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Isotopes</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers (different neutron numbers).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: ¹²C and ¹⁴C; ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl. Isotopes have identical chemical properties but different physical properties.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Electronic Configuration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Electrons fill shells around the nucleus: Shell 1 = max 2; Shell 2 = max 8; Shell 3 = max 8 (for first 20 elements); Shell 4 = 2.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example — Sodium (Z=11): 2, 8, 1. Sodium has 1 valence electron.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Atomic number = protons = electrons (neutral atom). Mass number = protons + neutrons. Neutrons = A − Z. Isotopes: same Z, different A. Valence electrons determine chemical reactivity.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Atoms have protons (+1) and neutrons (0) in the nucleus, electrons (−1) in shells. Atomic number = protons; mass number = protons + neutrons. Isotopes share the same Z but differ in A. Electrons fill shells: 2, 8, 8, 2 for the first 20 elements.\n</div>\n  ",
