    "topic": "Chemical Combination",
    "topicCode": "SS1-CHEM-07",
    "module": "Chemical Laws",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Chemical combination</span> refers to the rules governing how elements combine to form compounds. The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">laws of chemical combination</span> were established through careful experimentation and form the foundation of stoichiometry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Law of Conservation of Mass (Lavoisier, 1789)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"Matter can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction — the total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products.\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Mass of H₂ + mass of O₂ = mass of H₂O produced.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Law of Definite Proportions (Proust, 1799)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"A compound always contains the same elements in the same fixed mass ratio regardless of source.\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> Water (H₂O) always contains H and O in the mass ratio 1:8.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Law of Multiple Proportions (Dalton, 1803)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"When two elements A and B form more than one compound, the masses of B combining with a fixed mass of A are in simple whole-number ratios.\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> CO and CO₂: In CO, 12g C + 16g O. In CO₂, 12g C + 32g O. Ratio of O = 16:32 = 1:2.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Law of Combining Volumes (Gay-Lussac, 1808)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"Gases react in volumes that are in simple whole-number ratios at the same temperature and pressure.\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Volume ratio = 1:1:2.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Avogadro's Law (Avogadro, 1811)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules.\"</em></p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Conservation of mass = Lavoisier. Definite proportions = Proust. Multiple proportions = Dalton. Combining volumes = Gay-Lussac. Avogadro's law = Avogadro. Multiple proportions always gives simple whole-number ratios.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Five laws: (1) Conservation of mass — total mass conserved. (2) Definite proportions — fixed mass ratios. (3) Multiple proportions — simple whole-number ratios. (4) Combining volumes — gases react in simple volume ratios. (5) Avogadro — equal volumes contain equal numbers of molecules.\n</div>\n  ",
