    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Acids</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">bases</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">salts</span> are fundamental classes of chemical compounds encountered in everyday life — from citric acid in oranges, hydrochloric acid in the stomach, to sodium hydroxide in soap and calcium carbonate in chalk.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Acids</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <strong>acid</strong> donates hydrogen ions (H⁺) in solution (Arrhenius) or is a proton donor (Brønsted-Lowry).</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Properties:</strong> Sour taste; turn blue litmus red; pH &lt; 7; react with metals to produce H₂ gas; react with carbonates to produce CO₂; neutralise bases.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Common acids:</strong> HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃, CH₃COOH (ethanoic acid/vinegar), citric acid.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Bases and Alkalis</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <strong>base</strong> accepts H⁺ ions. An <strong>alkali</strong> is a base soluble in water.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Properties:</strong> Bitter taste; soapy feel; turn red litmus blue; pH &gt; 7; neutralise acids; react with ammonium salts releasing NH₃.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Common examples:</strong> NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂ (limewater), NH₃(aq).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. pH Scale</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>pH 0–6: Acidic (lower = more acidic)</li>\n  <li>pH 7: Neutral (pure water)</li>\n  <li>pH 8–14: Alkaline (higher = more alkaline)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Neutralisation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example: HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example: H₂SO₄ + 2KOH → K₂SO₄ + 2H₂O</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Acids turn blue litmus red; alkalis turn red litmus blue. pH &lt; 7 = acid; pH 7 = neutral; pH &gt; 7 = alkali. Neutralisation always produces salt + water. Strong acids (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) fully ionise; weak acids (CH₃COOH) partially ionise.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Acids: donate H⁺, sour taste, blue litmus → red, pH &lt; 7. Bases: accept H⁺, bitter taste, soapy feel, pH &gt; 7. Alkalis = soluble bases. Neutralisation = acid + base → salt + water.\n</div>\n  ",
