    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Chemical symbols</span> are shorthand representations of elements. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Chemical formulae</span> show the composition of compounds. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Chemical equations</span> describe reactions — these are the language of chemistry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Chemical Symbols</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Each element uses a 1–2 letter symbol (first letter always capitalised). Many symbols derive from Latin names.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Latin Origin</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sodium</td><td>Na</td><td>Natrium</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Potassium</td><td>K</td><td>Kalium</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iron</td><td>Fe</td><td>Ferrum</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Copper</td><td>Cu</td><td>Cuprum</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Silver</td><td>Ag</td><td>Argentum</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gold</td><td>Au</td><td>Aurum</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lead</td><td>Pb</td><td>Plumbum</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>Hg</td><td>Hydrargyrum</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Chemical Formulae</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Subscript numbers show how many atoms of each element are present. Examples: H₂O (2H, 1O); H₂SO₄ (2H, 1S, 4O); Ca(OH)₂ (1Ca, 2O, 2H); Al₂(SO₄)₃ (2Al, 3S, 12O).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Balancing Chemical Equations</h3>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Write the unbalanced formula equation.</li>\n  <li>Count atoms on each side.</li>\n  <li>Add coefficients (never change subscripts) to balance.</li>\n  <li>Verify by counting atoms again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Left: 4H, 2O. Right: 4H, 2O ✓</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. State Symbols</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">(s) = solid; (l) = liquid; (g) = gas; (aq) = aqueous solution</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Never change subscripts when balancing — only change coefficients. Know Latin-origin symbols: Na (Natrium), K (Kalium), Fe (Ferrum), Cu (Cuprum), Ag (Argentum), Au (Aurum), Pb (Plumbum), Hg (Hydrargyrum).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Symbols use 1–2 letters with first letter capitalised. Formulae show atom types and numbers. Balanced equations have equal atoms on both sides. State symbols: (s), (l), (g), (aq).\n</div>\n  ",
