    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Qualitative analysis</span> identifies <em>what</em> substances are present in a sample. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Quantitative analysis</span> determines <em>how much</em> of a substance is present. Both are fundamental skills in chemistry, medicine, environmental science, and industry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Qualitative Analysis — Basic Tests</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Procedure</th><th>Positive Result</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Test for CO₂</td><td>Bubble gas through limewater</td><td>Limewater turns milky (CaCO₃ precipitate)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Test for H₂</td><td>Apply burning splint to gas</td><td>Squeaky pop</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Test for O₂</td><td>Apply glowing splint to gas</td><td>Glowing splint relights</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Test for NH₃</td><td>Moist red litmus paper</td><td>Red litmus turns blue; pungent smell</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Test for Cl₂</td><td>Moist blue litmus paper</td><td>Turns red then bleaches white</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Test for water</td><td>Anhydrous cobalt chloride paper</td><td>Blue → pink</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Quantitative Analysis — Titration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Titration is the main quantitative technique. A solution of known concentration (standard solution) is used to find the concentration of an unknown solution.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Key terms:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Titre:</strong> The volume of solution delivered from the burette to reach the endpoint.</li>\n  <li><strong>Endpoint:</strong> The point at which the indicator changes colour permanently.</li>\n  <li><strong>Equivalence point:</strong> The theoretical point at which moles of acid = moles of base (adjusted for stoichiometry).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Indicators</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Methyl orange:</strong> Red in acid, yellow in alkali. Best for strong acid–strong base or strong acid–weak base.</li>\n  <li><strong>Phenolphthalein:</strong> Colourless in acid, pink/purple in alkali. Best for weak acid–strong base.</li>\n  <li><strong>Litmus:</strong> Red in acid, blue in alkali.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Qualitative = identity (WHAT). Quantitative = amount (HOW MUCH). Know all gas tests. For titration: Methyl orange is best for HCl/NaOH; Phenolphthalein best for weak acid/strong base. Anhydrous CoCl₂ = blue → pink for water detection.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Qualitative analysis identifies substances using tests (e.g., limewater for CO₂, burning splint for H₂). Quantitative analysis measures amounts — titration being the main method. Indicators show the endpoint: methyl orange (red→yellow) and phenolphthalein (colourless→pink).\n</div>\n  ",
