    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">mole</span> is the SI unit for amount of substance. One mole contains exactly <span class=\"learn-keyword\">6.02 × 10²³ particles</span> (Avogadro's number). The mole concept links the macroscopic (grams) to the microscopic (atoms/molecules) world, making quantitative calculations possible.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Definitions</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Mole (mol):</strong> The amount of substance containing 6.02 × 10²³ particles (atoms, molecules, or ions).</li>\n  <li><strong>Avogadro's number (Nₐ):</strong> 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.</li>\n  <li><strong>Molar mass (M):</strong> The mass of one mole of a substance in g/mol. Numerically equal to the relative atomic/molecular mass.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Mole Calculations</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Number of moles = mass ÷ molar mass</strong><br>n = m ÷ M</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Number of particles = moles × Avogadro's number</strong><br>N = n × Nₐ</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> How many moles in 9g of water (M = 18 g/mol)?<br>n = 9 ÷ 18 = 0.5 mol</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Molar Mass of Compounds</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Add the relative atomic masses (RAM) of all atoms in the formula.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> Molar mass of CaCO₃: Ca(40) + C(12) + 3×O(16) = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g/mol.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Stoichiometry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Stoichiometry uses the balanced equation to find the mole ratios of reactants and products.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 2 mol H₂ reacts with 1 mol O₂ to form 2 mol H₂O (2:1:2 ratio).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key formula: n = m/M. Always use molar mass (g/mol) in calculations. Avogadro's number = 6.02 × 10²³. To find number of particles: N = n × Nₐ. Molar mass of H₂O = 18; NaCl = 58.5; CaCO₃ = 100; H₂SO₄ = 98.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> 1 mole = 6.02 × 10²³ particles. n = m/M. Stoichiometry uses mole ratios from balanced equations to link reactants and products. Molar mass = sum of all RAM in the formula.\n</div>\n  ",
