    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Percentage composition</span> tells us the mass percentage of each element in a compound. The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">empirical formula</span> gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound, while the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">molecular formula</span> gives the actual number of each atom.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Percentage Composition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>% of element = (mass of element in formula ÷ molar mass of compound) × 100</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> % of H in H₂O (M=18): = (2÷18) × 100 = 11.1%</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">% of O in H₂O: = (16÷18) × 100 = 88.9%</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Empirical Formula</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Steps to find the empirical formula from percentage composition:</p>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Assume 100g of the compound — percentages become grams.</li>\n  <li>Divide each mass by the RAM of the element to get moles.</li>\n  <li>Divide all mole values by the smallest mole value.</li>\n  <li>Round to the nearest whole number to get the ratio.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> A compound contains 40% C, 6.7% H, 53.3% O.<br>C: 40/12 = 3.33; H: 6.7/1 = 6.7; O: 53.3/16 = 3.33<br>Divide by smallest (3.33): C=1, H=2, O=1 → Empirical formula: <strong>CH₂O</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Molecular Formula from Empirical Formula</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>n = Molar mass of compound ÷ Molar mass of empirical formula</strong><br>Molecular formula = n × empirical formula</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> If empirical formula CH₂O has molar mass 30, and actual molar mass is 180, then n = 180/30 = 6. Molecular formula = C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> For empirical formula: % → grams → moles (÷ RAM) → simplest ratio (÷ smallest). The empirical formula may equal the molecular formula (e.g., NaCl, H₂O). Molecular formula = n × empirical formula, where n = molar mass ÷ empirical formula mass.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> % composition = (element mass/molar mass) × 100. Empirical formula = simplest atom ratio (from % data: divide by RAM, then by smallest value). Molecular formula = n × empirical formula.\n</div>\n  ",
