    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">gas laws</span> describe the relationships between pressure (P), volume (V), and temperature (T) for a fixed amount of an ideal gas. These laws form the basis for understanding gas behaviour and are widely applied in industry, engineering, and chemistry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Boyle's Law (Robert Boyle, 1662)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"At constant temperature, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its pressure.\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</strong> (at constant T and n)</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> A gas occupies 5 dm³ at 100 kPa. What is its volume at 200 kPa (constant T)?<br>V₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ P₂ = (100 × 5) ÷ 200 = 2.5 dm³</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Charles's Law (Jacques Charles, 1787)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"At constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature (Kelvin).\"</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</strong> (at constant P and n)</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Note: Temperature must be in Kelvin: K = °C + 273</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Ideal Gas Equation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Combining Boyle's and Charles's Laws gives the <strong>Ideal Gas Equation:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>PV = nRT</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">where P = pressure (Pa), V = volume (m³), n = moles, R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, T = temperature (K).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Combined Gas Law</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Used when both pressure and temperature change simultaneously.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always convert temperature to Kelvin (K = °C + 273) before using gas law equations. Boyle's: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂. Charles's: V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂. Combined: P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂. Ideal gas: PV = nRT.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Gas laws: Boyle's (P inversely proportional to V at constant T); Charles's (V directly proportional to T at constant P); Combined (P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂); Ideal gas equation (PV = nRT).\n</div>\n  ",
