    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Building on basic atomic structure, this topic explores <span class=\"learn-keyword\">atomic models</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">electron shells and sub-shells</span>, and how electronic configuration determines an element's position in the Periodic Table and its chemical behaviour.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Development of Atomic Models</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Dalton (1803):</strong> Solid sphere model — atoms are indivisible solid spheres.</li>\n  <li><strong>Thomson (1904):</strong> Plum pudding model — electrons embedded in a positive sphere.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rutherford (1911):</strong> Nuclear model — small dense positive nucleus with electrons orbiting it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bohr (1913):</strong> Planetary model — electrons orbit in fixed energy shells (energy levels).</li>\n  <li><strong>Modern quantum model:</strong> Electrons exist in orbitals (probability clouds) within sub-shells.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Rutherford's Gold Foil Experiment</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Alpha particles were fired at thin gold foil. Observations:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Most particles passed straight through (atom is mostly empty space).</li>\n  <li>A few were deflected at large angles (positive nucleus exists).</li>\n  <li>Very few bounced straight back (nucleus is small and dense).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Electronic Configuration Rules</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Shell 1 (K): max 2 electrons</li>\n  <li>Shell 2 (L): max 8 electrons</li>\n  <li>Shell 3 (M): max 8 electrons (for first 20 elements)</li>\n  <li>Shell 4 (N): max 2 electrons (for first 20 elements)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Electrons fill from the innermost shell outwards.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Electronic Configuration and the Periodic Table</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The number of valence electrons equals the group number (main group). The number of occupied shells equals the period number.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Z</th><th>Config</th><th>Period</th><th>Group</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Na</td><td>11</td><td>2,8,1</td><td>3</td><td>I</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mg</td><td>12</td><td>2,8,2</td><td>3</td><td>II</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cl</td><td>17</td><td>2,8,7</td><td>3</td><td>VII</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ca</td><td>20</td><td>2,8,8,2</td><td>4</td><td>II</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Rutherford's experiment proved atoms have a small, dense, positive nucleus. Bohr added fixed energy shells. Configuration: 2,8,8,2 for Ca (Z=20). Group = valence electrons; Period = number of shells.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Atomic models evolved from Dalton → Thomson → Rutherford → Bohr → quantum model. Rutherford's gold foil experiment revealed a small dense nucleus. Electronic configuration fills shells 2,8,8,2 for the first 20 elements.\n</div>\n  ",
