    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Chemical bonds form when atoms interact to achieve a more stable electron configuration — typically a full outer shell of 8 electrons (<span class=\"learn-keyword\">octet rule</span>). The two main types of bonding are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ionic bonding</span> (electron transfer) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">covalent bonding</span> (electron sharing).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Ionic Bonding</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Ionic bonds form by the <strong>transfer of electrons</strong> from a metal to a non-metal. The metal loses electrons to form a positive ion (cation); the non-metal gains electrons to form a negative ion (anion). The oppositely charged ions attract each other electrostatically.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> NaCl — Na loses 1e⁻ → Na⁺; Cl gains 1e⁻ → Cl⁻. The Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions attract to form the ionic lattice.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Properties of Ionic Compounds</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>High melting and boiling points (strong electrostatic forces)</li>\n  <li>Conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water (ions are free to move)</li>\n  <li>Do not conduct electricity when solid (ions fixed in lattice)</li>\n  <li>Usually soluble in water; insoluble in organic solvents</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Covalent Bonding</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Covalent bonds form by the <strong>sharing of electrons</strong> between non-metals. Each shared pair of electrons forms one covalent bond.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Single bond:</strong> 1 shared pair (e.g., H–H, H–Cl)</li>\n  <li><strong>Double bond:</strong> 2 shared pairs (e.g., O=O, C=O)</li>\n  <li><strong>Triple bond:</strong> 3 shared pairs (e.g., N≡N)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Properties of Covalent Compounds</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Generally low melting and boiling points (weak intermolecular forces)</li>\n  <li>Do not conduct electricity (no free ions or electrons)</li>\n  <li>Usually soluble in organic solvents; insoluble in water (non-polar)</li>\n  <li>Can be gases, liquids, or solids at room temperature</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Ionic = metal + non-metal = electron transfer. Covalent = non-metal + non-metal = electron sharing. Ionic compounds conduct when molten/dissolved; covalent compounds generally do not. High mp/bp = ionic; low mp/bp = simple covalent.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Ionic bonds: electron transfer; metal→cation, non-metal→anion; high mp/bp; conduct when molten. Covalent bonds: electron sharing between non-metals; single/double/triple bonds; low mp/bp; do not conduct electricity.\n</div>\n  ",
