    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Water is one of the most important and abundant substances on Earth. It is called the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">universal solvent</span> due to its remarkable ability to dissolve a wide variety of substances. Understanding the properties of water and solutions is fundamental to chemistry, biology, and environmental science.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Properties of Water</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Physical:</strong> Colourless, odourless, tasteless liquid at room temperature. Boiling point = 100°C; melting point = 0°C at 1 atm.</li>\n  <li><strong>Polarity:</strong> Water is a polar molecule (bent shape, O is δ⁻, H is δ⁺). This polarity gives it unique properties.</li>\n  <li><strong>High specific heat capacity:</strong> Water can absorb large amounts of heat without a large temperature change.</li>\n  <li><strong>High surface tension:</strong> Due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Density anomaly:</strong> Ice (solid) is less dense than liquid water — ice floats.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Water as a Solvent</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Water dissolves substances by surrounding solute particles with water molecules (hydration). It dissolves ionic compounds and polar covalent compounds but not non-polar substances.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Solutions — Key Terms</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Solution:</strong> A homogeneous mixture of solute dissolved in solvent.</li>\n  <li><strong>Solute:</strong> The substance dissolved.</li>\n  <li><strong>Solvent:</strong> The substance doing the dissolving.</li>\n  <li><strong>Concentration:</strong> Amount of solute per unit volume of solution (mol/dm³ or g/dm³).</li>\n  <li><strong>Saturated solution:</strong> A solution containing the maximum amount of dissolved solute at a given temperature.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Hard and Soft Water</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Hard water</strong> contains dissolved Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions (from limestone rocks) that prevent soap from lathering easily. It can be temporary (removed by boiling) or permanent (removed by ion exchange or adding Na₂CO₃).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Water is polar → it dissolves ionic and polar compounds (\"like dissolves like\"). Hard water: Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions. Temporary hardness caused by Ca(HCO₃)₂ — removed by boiling. Permanent hardness (CaSO₄) — not removed by boiling; use ion exchange or Na₂CO₃.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Water is a polar molecule with high specific heat capacity and hydrogen bonding. It is the universal solvent dissolving ionic and polar substances. Hard water contains Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ ions causing soap wastage. Concentration = moles ÷ volume.\n</div>\n  ",
