    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Solubility</span> is the maximum mass of solute that dissolves in 100g of solvent at a specified temperature. <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Crystallisation</span> is the process of obtaining pure solid crystals from a solution. Both concepts are central to purification processes in chemistry and industry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Solubility</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Solubility is usually expressed in grams of solute per 100g of solvent at a given temperature.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>For most solids: solubility <strong>increases with temperature</strong>.</li>\n  <li>For gases: solubility <strong>decreases with temperature</strong> (e.g., CO₂ in soft drinks).</li>\n  <li>A <strong>saturated solution</strong> contains the maximum dissolved solute at that temperature.</li>\n  <li>A <strong>supersaturated solution</strong> contains more dissolved solute than a saturated solution (unstable; crystals form easily).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Solubility Curves</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A solubility curve is a graph showing how solubility changes with temperature. Most solid solutes show increasing solubility with temperature. The point on the curve gives the solubility at that temperature.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Crystallisation Process</h3>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Dissolve the solid in hot water to make a concentrated (nearly saturated) solution.</li>\n  <li>Filter to remove insoluble impurities.</li>\n  <li>Evaporate gently (on a water bath) until crystals begin to form on the surface.</li>\n  <li>Allow to cool slowly — crystals grow as the substance becomes less soluble at lower temperatures.</li>\n  <li>Filter to collect the crystals; dry with filter paper or in an oven.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Water of Crystallisation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Some ionic compounds crystallise with a fixed number of water molecules incorporated into their crystal structure. These are called <strong>hydrated salts</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue copper sulphate); Na₂CO₃·10H₂O (washing soda); CaCl₂·6H₂O.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Solubility of solids increases with temperature; gases decrease with temperature. Crystallisation purifies by growing crystals that exclude impurities. CuSO₄·5H₂O contains 5 molecules of water of crystallisation — the dot (·) separates the salt formula from the water.</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Solubility = max grams dissolving per 100g solvent at given temperature. Solids become more soluble with higher temperature; gases less soluble. Crystallisation: dissolve → filter → evaporate → cool → filter crystals. Hydrated salts contain water of crystallisation.\n</div>\n  ",
