    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Alkanes</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">alkenes</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">alkynes</span> are the three main families of hydrocarbons. Their properties and reactions differ significantly based on whether they contain single, double, or triple carbon-carbon bonds.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Alkanes — Saturated Hydrocarbons</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Alkanes have only C−C single bonds (saturated). They are relatively unreactive but undergo combustion and substitution reactions.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th><th>State at RTP</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Methane</td><td>CH₄</td><td>Gas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ethane</td><td>C₂H₆</td><td>Gas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Propane</td><td>C₃H₈</td><td>Gas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Butane</td><td>C₄H₁₀</td><td>Gas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pentane</td><td>C₅H₁₂</td><td>Liquid</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Combustion of alkanes:</strong> CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O (complete); produces CO and H₂O if incomplete.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Substitution (halogenation):</strong> In UV light, alkanes react with halogens: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Alkenes — Unsaturated Hydrocarbons</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Alkenes have at least one C=C double bond. They are more reactive than alkanes due to the double bond.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Addition reaction:</strong> Alkenes add molecules across the double bond.<br>C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆ (hydrogenation)<br>C₂H₄ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br (decolourises bromine water — test for alkene)</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Alkynes — Triple Bond Hydrocarbons</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Alkynes have a C≡C triple bond. Ethyne (C₂H₂, acetylene) is the most important. Used in welding (oxy-acetylene torch). Also undergo addition reactions.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Test for alkenes: add bromine water — it decolourises (orange → colourless). Alkanes do NOT decolourise bromine water. Complete combustion = CO₂ + H₂O. Incomplete combustion = CO + H₂O (or soot/carbon).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Alkanes: single bonds, saturated, relatively unreactive — combustion and substitution. Alkenes: double bond, unsaturated, reactive — addition reactions, decolourise bromine water. Alkynes: triple bond — addition reactions; ethyne used in welding.\n</div>\n  ",
