    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Isomers</span> are compounds that have the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">same molecular formula</span> but <span class=\"learn-keyword\">different structural arrangements</span> of atoms. Isomerism explains why so many organic compounds are possible and why compounds with the same formula can have different properties.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Structural Isomerism</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different connectivity (different structural formulae). Types include:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Chain isomerism:</strong> Different carbon chain arrangements (straight chain vs branched). Example: butane and methylpropane (both C₄H₁₀).</li>\n  <li><strong>Position isomerism:</strong> Same functional group at different positions. Example: 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane (both C₃H₇Br).</li>\n  <li><strong>Functional group isomerism:</strong> Different functional groups. Example: ethanol (C₂H₅OH) and methoxymethane (CH₃OCH₃) — both C₂H₆O.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Chain Isomers of Butane (C₄H₁₀)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>n-Butane:</strong> CH₃−CH₂−CH₂−CH₃ (straight chain)</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>2-Methylpropane (isobutane):</strong> CH₃−CH(CH₃)−CH₃ (branched)</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Both have molecular formula C₄H₁₀ but different structures and slightly different physical properties (different boiling points).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Why Isomers Have Different Properties</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms. This leads to different physical properties (boiling points, melting points, solubility) and sometimes different chemical properties if they have different functional groups.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Isomers = same molecular formula, different structural arrangement. Three types: chain isomers (different chain shape), position isomers (functional group at different position), functional group isomers (different functional group). Always check the molecular formula is the same.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Isomers share the same molecular formula but differ in structure. Types: chain isomers, position isomers, functional group isomers. Structural isomers may have different physical and/or chemical properties.\n</div>\n  ",
