    "module": "Industrial Chemistry",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Petroleum</span> (crude oil) is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons formed over millions of years from the remains of marine organisms. It is refined by <span class=\"learn-keyword\">fractional distillation</span> into useful fractions, and these fractions form the basis of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">petrochemicals</span> — thousands of useful products.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Origin of Petroleum</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Petroleum formed from the remains of tiny marine organisms (plankton) buried under sediment over millions of years. Heat and pressure transformed the organic matter into crude oil and natural gas (fossil fuels).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Fractional Distillation of Crude Oil</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Crude oil is heated and fed into a fractionating column where different fractions condense at different levels.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Fraction</th><th>Approx boiling range</th><th>Uses</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Petroleum gases</td><td>Below 25°C</td><td>LPG (cooking, heating)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Petrol (gasoline)</td><td>25–75°C</td><td>Motor fuel</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Naphtha</td><td>75–160°C</td><td>Chemical feedstock</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Kerosene</td><td>160–250°C</td><td>Jet fuel, paraffin</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Diesel</td><td>250–350°C</td><td>Lorries, buses, trains</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fuel oil</td><td>350–400°C</td><td>Ships, power stations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bitumen</td><td>Above 400°C</td><td>Road surfacing, roofing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Cracking</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Cracking</strong> breaks down large hydrocarbon molecules (from high-boiling fractions) into smaller, more useful molecules — especially alkenes and petrol-range alkanes. It uses high temperature and/or a catalyst.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example: C₁₆H₃₄ → C₈H₁₈ + C₄H₈ + C₄H₈</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Petrochemicals</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Petrochemicals are chemicals derived from petroleum or natural gas. Examples: ethene (for plastics), propene (for polypropylene), benzene (for dyes and medicines), methanol.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> In fractional distillation, temperature decreases from bottom to top. Low boiling fractions (gases, petrol) rise to the top; high boiling fractions (bitumen) stay at the bottom. Cracking converts large molecules to small, useful ones including alkenes.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Petroleum is a mixture of hydrocarbons separated by fractional distillation. Fractions include gases, petrol, naphtha, kerosene, diesel, fuel oil, and bitumen. Cracking breaks large molecules into smaller useful ones. Petrochemicals are chemicals made from petroleum.\n</div>\n  ",
