    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">chemical reaction</span> involves the rearrangement of atoms to form new substances with different properties. Reactions can be classified into several types based on what happens to the reactants and products.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Chemical Reactions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Combination/Synthesis</td><td>Two or more substances combine to form one product</td><td>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decomposition</td><td>One substance breaks down into two or more simpler substances</td><td>2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Displacement</td><td>A more reactive element displaces a less reactive one from its compound</td><td>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Precipitation</td><td>Two solutions react to form an insoluble solid (precipitate)</td><td>AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Neutralisation</td><td>Acid + base → salt + water</td><td>HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Combustion</td><td>Substance reacts with oxygen, releasing energy</td><td>CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Redox</td><td>Simultaneous oxidation and reduction</td><td>Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Signs of a Chemical Reaction</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Change in colour</li>\n  <li>Formation of a gas (effervescence)</li>\n  <li>Formation of a precipitate</li>\n  <li>Change in temperature (heat absorbed or released)</li>\n  <li>Change in smell</li>\n  <li>Formation of light or electricity</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Reversible and Irreversible Reactions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <strong>irreversible reaction</strong> goes to completion in one direction. An <strong>reversible reaction</strong> can proceed in both directions and is shown with a double arrow (⇌).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know all 7 types of chemical reactions with examples. Displacement requires a more reactive element displacing a less reactive one. Precipitation forms an insoluble product (↓). Neutralisation always gives salt + water.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Types of reactions: combination, decomposition, displacement, precipitation, neutralisation, combustion, and redox. Signs of chemical change include gas evolution, precipitate, colour change, and temperature change.\n</div>\n  ",
