    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  All chemical reactions involve <span class=\"learn-keyword\">energy changes</span>. Reactions that release energy to the surroundings are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">exothermic</span>; those that absorb energy from the surroundings are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">endothermic</span>. Energy changes are measured in kilojoules per mole (kJ/mol).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Exothermic Reactions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Exothermic reactions release energy (heat) to the surroundings. The products have less energy than the reactants. Temperature of surroundings increases.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>ΔH is negative</strong> for exothermic reactions.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Examples:</strong> Combustion, neutralisation, respiration, rusting of iron, condensation, freezing.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Endothermic Reactions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Endothermic reactions absorb energy from the surroundings. The products have more energy than the reactants. Temperature of surroundings decreases.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>ΔH is positive</strong> for endothermic reactions.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Examples:</strong> Thermal decomposition, photosynthesis, dissolving ammonium nitrate in water, melting ice.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Energy Level Diagrams</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Energy level (profile) diagrams show the energy of reactants and products:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Exothermic:</strong> Products are at a lower energy level than reactants. ΔH = negative.</li>\n  <li><strong>Endothermic:</strong> Products are at a higher energy level than reactants. ΔH = positive.</li>\n  <li><strong>Activation energy (Eₐ):</strong> The minimum energy required to start a reaction — shown as the hump in the energy profile diagram.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Bond Breaking and Bond Making</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Energy is <strong>absorbed</strong> when bonds are broken. Energy is <strong>released</strong> when bonds are made. If energy released (bond making) > energy absorbed (bond breaking) → exothermic overall.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Exothermic: releases heat, ΔH negative, surroundings get warmer. Endothermic: absorbs heat, ΔH positive, surroundings get cooler. Bond breaking = absorbs energy; bond making = releases energy. Activation energy = minimum energy to start the reaction.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Exothermic (ΔH < 0): releases heat — combustion, neutralisation, respiration. Endothermic (ΔH > 0): absorbs heat — decomposition, photosynthesis. Bond breaking absorbs energy; bond making releases energy. Activation energy = energy barrier to start the reaction.\n</div>\n  ",
