    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">rate of a chemical reaction</span> measures how fast reactants are converted to products. Understanding reaction rates is important for controlling industrial processes, food preservation, medicine, and everyday chemistry.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Definition of Reaction Rate</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Rate of reaction = change in concentration (or amount) of reactant or product ÷ time taken.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Rate can be measured by: decrease in mass of reactant, increase in gas volume, change in colour or turbidity.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Factors Affecting Reaction Rate</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on rate</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Increases</td><td>More kinetic energy → more frequent, more energetic collisions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concentration</td><td>Increases</td><td>More particles per unit volume → more frequent collisions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pressure (gases)</td><td>Increases</td><td>Higher pressure = higher concentration of gas molecules</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Surface area</td><td>Increases</td><td>More exposed surface = more collision opportunities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Catalyst</td><td>Increases</td><td>Provides alternative pathway with lower activation energy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Light</td><td>Can increase</td><td>Provides energy for photochemical reactions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Collision Theory</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">For a reaction to occur, reactant particles must:</p>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Collide with each other (collision must occur)</li>\n  <li>Have energy ≥ activation energy (successful collision)</li>\n  <li>Have the correct orientation (for complex molecules)</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A reaction can only occur if there is a <strong>successful collision</strong> — one with sufficient energy (≥ Eₐ) and correct orientation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Catalysts</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A catalyst increases reaction rate by providing an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. The catalyst is not consumed in the reaction. Examples: MnO₂ catalyses H₂O₂ decomposition; Fe catalyses the Haber process; enzymes in biological systems.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> All factors that increase rate do so by increasing the frequency or energy of collisions. Temperature is the most effective: even a 10°C rise roughly doubles the reaction rate. Catalysts don't change ΔH — they only lower activation energy.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Rate of reaction depends on temperature, concentration, pressure, surface area, catalysts, and light. Collision theory: successful collisions require sufficient energy (≥ Eₐ) and correct orientation. Catalysts lower Eₐ without being consumed.\n</div>\n  ",
