    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Some chemical reactions are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">reversible</span> — they can proceed in both the forward and reverse directions. When the rates of the forward and reverse reactions become equal, the system reaches <span class=\"learn-keyword\">dynamic equilibrium</span>. Understanding equilibrium is essential for industrial processes like the Haber process.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Reversible Reactions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A reversible reaction can proceed in both directions. It is shown with a double arrow: A + B ⇌ C + D.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) (Haber process)</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Example:</strong> CuSO₄·5H₂O ⇌ CuSO₄ + 5H₂O (on heating/cooling)</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Dynamic Equilibrium</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">At dynamic equilibrium:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The rate of the forward reaction = rate of the reverse reaction.</li>\n  <li>Concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (but not necessarily equal).</li>\n  <li>The reaction appears to have stopped but both forward and reverse reactions are still occurring.</li>\n  <li>It is a dynamic (not static) equilibrium — both reactions continue simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Le Chatelier's Principle</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><em>\"If a change is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system will respond in a way that opposes the change and re-establishes equilibrium.\"</em></p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Change applied</th><th>System response</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Increase concentration of reactant</td><td>Equilibrium shifts forward (→) to use up extra reactant</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Increase temperature</td><td>Equilibrium shifts in endothermic direction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Increase pressure (gas reactions)</td><td>Equilibrium shifts toward side with fewer gas moles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Add catalyst</td><td>Reaches equilibrium faster; yield unchanged</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Dynamic equilibrium: forward rate = reverse rate; concentrations constant. Le Chatelier: system opposes the change. For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (ΔH = −92 kJ/mol): high pressure → more NH₃; low temperature → more NH₃ (but slower rate). Catalyst reaches equilibrium faster, not more NH₃.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Reversible reactions shown with ⇌. Dynamic equilibrium: both reactions ongoing at equal rates; concentrations constant. Le Chatelier's principle: system opposes applied change. Haber process: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (compromise conditions: ~450°C, 200 atm, Fe catalyst).\n</div>\n  ",
