    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Elements in the Periodic Table are broadly classified as <span class=\"learn-keyword\">metals</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">non-metals</span>, or metalloids (semi-metals). Understanding the properties and reactions of metals and non-metals is fundamental to inorganic chemistry and has wide practical applications.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Properties of Metals</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Good conductors of heat and electricity</li>\n  <li>Malleable (can be beaten into sheets) and ductile (can be drawn into wires)</li>\n  <li>Lustrous (shiny) when polished</li>\n  <li>High melting and boiling points (exceptions: Na, K are soft; Hg is liquid)</li>\n  <li>High density (exceptions: Na, K, Li are less dense than water)</li>\n  <li>Form positive ions (cations) by losing electrons</li>\n  <li>Form ionic/basic oxides</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Properties of Non-Metals</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Generally poor conductors of heat and electricity (exception: graphite)</li>\n  <li>Brittle as solids (not malleable or ductile)</li>\n  <li>Generally low melting and boiling points</li>\n  <li>Form negative ions (anions) by gaining electrons</li>\n  <li>Form acidic or neutral oxides</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Reactivity Series of Metals</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The reactivity series arranges metals in decreasing order of reactivity:</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">K > Na > Ca > Mg > Al > Zn > Fe > Pb > H > Cu > Ag > Au</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Memory aid:</strong> \"King Sol Called Me A Zealous Iron Provider Can't Apply\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Reactions of Metals</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>With water:</strong> K and Na react violently; Mg reacts with steam; Fe rusts; Cu, Au — no reaction.</li>\n  <li><strong>With dilute acid:</strong> Metals above H in activity series displace H₂. Cu, Ag, Au — no reaction.</li>\n  <li><strong>With oxygen:</strong> Most metals form metal oxides on heating.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Metals: conductors, malleable, ductile, form positive ions, basic oxides. Non-metals: poor conductors (except graphite), brittle, form negative ions, acidic oxides. Reactivity series: K>Na>Ca>Mg>Al>Zn>Fe>Pb>H>Cu>Ag>Au. Metals above H displace H₂ from acids.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Metals: conductors, malleable, ductile, form cations. Non-metals: poor conductors, brittle, form anions. Reactivity series determines which metals displace others. More reactive metals displace less reactive ones from solutions.\n</div>\n  ",
